{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01LM8AbQmpVnPJ5YifVCWZVD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DQByseUMYWjMmWedqeXdkf","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0519-3/projects/irt_personfit_contam/paper/sections/background.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5440,"cache_read_input_tokens":21947,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5440,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":36,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_013yzA5sjbJcJEm6UAx1yNFb","session_id":"38461102-0053-4fc0-9aca-3e348094d93e","uuid":"a9f99c0a-2f2c-4681-a2d5-c372e246de12"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01CWh9fNGAB89WekJ6ErFof5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01J8g3Zf8wCniHTdxWaid28j","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus: **novelty, positioning, related work, and presentation quality**.\n\n## Venue: EMNLP 2026 (via ARR)\nScoring scale (Overall Assessment, half-point increments 1–5):\n- 5 = Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference (Main track accept)\n- 3.5 = Borderline Conference\n- 3 = Findings\n- 2.5 = Borderline Findings\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\n\nDimensions: Soundness (1–5), Excitement (1–5), Reproducibility (1–5)\nAccept rate: ~21% for Main. Be calibrated accordingly.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title:** IRT-FIT: Detecting Benchmark Contamination in LLMs via Person-Fit Statistics on Binary Response Patterns\n\nThe paper adapts IRT person-fit statistics (ℓz*) to detect benchmark contamination in LLMs using only binary (correct/incorrect) response patterns — the \"minimal-information operating point\" on the detection frontier. 8 pages main text + references.\n\n**Three claimed contributions:**\n1. Minimal-information detection framework using ℓz* validated on 3 model families\n2. Detection characterization: power function (R²=0.62), 56% effect-size cost quantification, preliminary post-RL robustness\n3. Empirical findings: universal 4PL misfit across 12 models (57/60 cells)\n\n**Key method:** Compute ℓz* (Snijders 2001 corrected person-fit statistic) using pre-calibrated 4PL IRT parameters from PSN-IRT. Use Δℓz* (clean vs contaminated shift) as detection statistic. Novel analyses: θ-fixed to restore dose-response monotonicity, memorization fraction (MF), empirical power function.\n\n**Key results:**\n- |d|>2.0 in 2/3 families at 15% MMLU contamination (Qwen 2.27, Llama 2.09), Mistral 1.51\n- 56% effect-size cost vs token-prob methods (2.27 vs 5.20)\n- Post-RL: |d|=0.82 (partial retention) vs Min-K%++/PPL ≈0\n- HT statistic (also binary) achieves |d|=3.21-3.53, outperforming ℓz*\n- Item-level AUROC near-random (0.52-0.55)\n- Power function R²=0.62 across 7 benchmarks\n- Universal 4PL misfit: 57/60 model-benchmark cells flagged\n\n**Related work cited:** Brown et al. 2020 (n-gram), Shi et al. 2024 (Min-K%), Zhang et al. 2024 (Min-K%++), Deng et al. 2024 (Time Travel), Oren et al. 2024 (ConStat), CoDeC 2026, Wang et al. 2025 (post-RL failure), PSN-IRT 2026, IRT-LLM 2024, Drasgow et al. 1985, Snijders 2001, Hong et al. 2021 (multiscale lzm), Meijer & Sijtsma 2001.\n\n## Your Task (R2 — Novelty + Related Work + Presentation)\n\n### 1. Novelty Assessment\n- Is applying IRT person-fit statistics to LLM contamination detection genuinely novel? Or is it a straightforward application of existing psychometric tools to a new domain?\n- The θ-shift analysis — is this a significant methodological contribution or a known issue in person-fit literature?\n- The \"detection frontier\" / \"minimal-information operating point\" framing — does it constitute a conceptual contribution, or is it post-hoc rationalization of weaker performance?\n- The universal 4PL misfit finding (contribution 3) — is this surprising? LLMs are not human test-takers; wouldn't we expect IRT model misfit?\n- How does the novelty compare to what EMNLP 2026 Main expects?\n\n### 2. Related Work Completeness\n- Are there important missing references? Consider:\n  - Other person-fit statistics beyond ℓz* (e.g., U3, W, ECI indices) — some are mentioned as baselines but not thoroughly discussed\n  - IRT applications in NLP/LLM evaluation beyond PSN-IRT and IRT-LLM\n  - Other minimal-information contamination detection approaches\n  - Benchmark integrity / test security literature from educational measurement\n  - Recent contamination detection work (2024-2026)\n- Is Wang et al. [2025] properly contextualized? The post-RL claim heavily depends on this single reference.\n- Are the anonymous citations (PSN-IRT [2026], CoDeC [2026], IRT-LLM [2024]) potential anonymity violations?\n\n### 3. Positioning and Framing\n- The paper positions ℓz* as the primary method despite HT statistic achieving better |d| (3.21-3.53 vs 2.27/2.09) — is the justification (\"mechanistic interpretability\") convincing?\n- \"Minimal-information operating point\" — is this framing too generous? The method requires pre-calibrated 4PL parameters from PSN-IRT, which is itself a non-trivial dependency.\n- The 56% effect-size cost is highlighted as if it's acceptable — but is it? For practical contamination detection, would practitioners choose a weaker method?\n- The post-RL advantage is the strongest selling point, but it's based on N=1, 77% complete — is the paper over-selling this?\n\n### 4. Presentation Quality\n- Is the paper well-written and well-structured?\n- Are the tables and figures (described but I'll assess the textual descriptions) effective?\n- Is the 8-page limit respected? Does content feel compressed or padded?\n- Are claims appropriately hedged vs over-stated?\n- Are limitations adequately discussed?\n\n### 5. Broader Impact / Ethics\n- Any ethical concerns with a contamination detection tool?\n- Responsible NLP checklist considerations?\n\n### 6. Minor Issues\n- Reference formatting: \"PSN-IRT Authors\", \"CoDeC Authors\", \"IRT-LLM Authors\" — these are placeholder names, not proper anonymous citations\n- Any notation inconsistencies?\n\nProvide your assessment in structured format:\n- Summary (3-4 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, labeled W1, W2, ... with severity: major/minor)\n- Questions for Authors\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall Assessment: X/5 (use the scale above)\n- Confidence: X/5\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Calibrate to EMNLP 2026 acceptance rate (~21% Main, ~30% with Findings)."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":33679,"cache_read_input_tokens":23658,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":33679},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"38461102-0053-4fc0-9aca-3e348094d93e","uuid":"6f19f15d-ecbb-46d1-8fa1-0d4146dc42b3"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"ab8034bd95e94fdf5","tool_use_id":"toolu_01J8g3Zf8wCniHTdxWaid28j","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus: **novelty, positioning, related work, and presentation quality**.\n\n## Venue: EMNLP 2026 (via ARR)\nScoring scale (Overall Assessment, half-point increments 1–5):\n- 5 = Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference (Main track accept)\n- 3.5 = Borderline Conference\n- 3 = Findings\n- 2.5 = Borderline Findings\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\n\nDimensions: Soundness (1–5), Excitement (1–5), Reproducibility (1–5)\nAccept rate: ~21% for Main. Be calibrated accordingly.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title:** IRT-FIT: Detecting Benchmark Contamination in LLMs via Person-Fit Statistics on Binary Response Patterns\n\nThe paper adapts IRT person-fit statistics (ℓz*) to detect benchmark contamination in LLMs using only binary (correct/incorrect) response patterns — the \"minimal-information operating point\" on the detection frontier. 8 pages main text + references.\n\n**Three claimed contributions:**\n1. Minimal-information detection framework using ℓz* validated on 3 model families\n2. Detection characterization: power function (R²=0.62), 56% effect-size cost quantification, preliminary post-RL robustness\n3. Empirical findings: universal 4PL misfit across 12 models (57/60 cells)\n\n**Key method:** Compute ℓz* (Snijders 2001 corrected person-fit statistic) using pre-calibrated 4PL IRT parameters from PSN-IRT. Use Δℓz* (clean vs contaminated shift) as detection statistic. Novel analyses: θ-fixed to restore dose-response monotonicity, memorization fraction (MF), empirical power function.\n\n**Key results:**\n- |d|>2.0 in 2/3 families at 15% MMLU contamination (Qwen 2.27, Llama 2.09), Mistral 1.51\n- 56% effect-size cost vs token-prob methods (2.27 vs 5.20)\n- Post-RL: |d|=0.82 (partial retention) vs Min-K%++/PPL ≈0\n- HT statistic (also binary) achieves |d|=3.21-3.53, outperforming ℓz*\n- Item-level AUROC near-random (0.52-0.55)\n- Power function R²=0.62 across 7 benchmarks\n- Universal 4PL misfit: 57/60 model-benchmark cells flagged\n\n**Related work cited:** Brown et al. 2020 (n-gram), Shi et al. 2024 (Min-K%), Zhang et al. 2024 (Min-K%++), Deng et al. 2024 (Time Travel), Oren et al. 2024 (ConStat), CoDeC 2026, Wang et al. 2025 (post-RL failure), PSN-IRT 2026, IRT-LLM 2024, Drasgow et al. 1985, Snijders 2001, Hong et al. 2021 (multiscale lzm), Meijer & Sijtsma 2001.\n\n## Your Task (R2 — Novelty + Related Work + Presentation)\n\n### 1. Novelty Assessment\n- Is applying IRT person-fit statistics to LLM contamination detection genuinely novel? Or is it a straightforward application of existing psychometric tools to a new domain?\n- The θ-shift analysis — is this a significant methodological contribution or a known issue in person-fit literature?\n- The \"detection frontier\" / \"minimal-information operating point\" framing — does it constitute a conceptual contribution, or is it post-hoc rationalization of weaker performance?\n- The universal 4PL misfit finding (contribution 3) — is this surprising? LLMs are not human test-takers; wouldn't we expect IRT model misfit?\n- How does the novelty compare to what EMNLP 2026 Main expects?\n\n### 2. Related Work Completeness\n- Are there important missing references? Consider:\n  - Other person-fit statistics beyond ℓz* (e.g., U3, W, ECI indices) — some are mentioned as baselines but not thoroughly discussed\n  - IRT applications in NLP/LLM evaluation beyond PSN-IRT and IRT-LLM\n  - Other minimal-information contamination detection approaches\n  - Benchmark integrity / test security literature from educational measurement\n  - Recent contamination detection work (2024-2026)\n- Is Wang et al. [2025] properly contextualized? The post-RL claim heavily depends on this single reference.\n- Are the anonymous citations (PSN-IRT [2026], CoDeC [2026], IRT-LLM [2024]) potential anonymity violations?\n\n### 3. Positioning and Framing\n- The paper positions ℓz* as the primary method despite HT statistic achieving better |d| (3.21-3.53 vs 2.27/2.09) — is the justification (\"mechanistic interpretability\") convincing?\n- \"Minimal-information operating point\" — is this framing too generous? The method requires pre-calibrated 4PL parameters from PSN-IRT, which is itself a non-trivial dependency.\n- The 56% effect-size cost is highlighted as if it's acceptable — but is it? For practical contamination detection, would practitioners choose a weaker method?\n- The post-RL advantage is the strongest selling point, but it's based on N=1, 77% complete — is the paper over-selling this?\n\n### 4. Presentation Quality\n- Is the paper well-written and well-structured?\n- Are the tables and figures (described but I'll assess the textual descriptions) effective?\n- Is the 8-page limit respected? Does content feel compressed or padded?\n- Are claims appropriately hedged vs over-stated?\n- Are limitations adequately discussed?\n\n### 5. Broader Impact / Ethics\n- Any ethical concerns with a contamination detection tool?\n- Responsible NLP checklist considerations?\n\n### 6. Minor Issues\n- Reference formatting: \"PSN-IRT Authors\", \"CoDeC Authors\", \"IRT-LLM Authors\" — these are placeholder names, not proper anonymous citations\n- Any notation inconsistencies?\n\nProvide your assessment in structured format:\n- Summary (3-4 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, labeled W1, W2, ... with severity: major/minor)\n- Questions for Authors\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall Assessment: X/5 (use the scale above)\n- Confidence: X/5\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Calibrate to EMNLP 2026 acceptance rate (~21% Main, ~30% with Findings).","uuid":"03b96c1e-8416-4f90-a893-e6090fee4c3d","session_id":"38461102-0053-4fc0-9aca-3e348094d93e"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus: **novelty, positioning, related work, and presentation quality**.\n\n## Venue: EMNLP 2026 (via ARR)\nScoring scale (Overall Assessment, half-point increments 1–5):\n- 5 = Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference (Main track accept)\n- 3.5 = Borderline Conference\n- 3 = Findings\n- 2.5 = Borderline Findings\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\n\nDimensions: Soundness (1–5), Excitement (1–5), Reproducibility (1–5)\nAccept rate: ~21% for Main. Be calibrated accordingly.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title:** IRT-FIT: Detecting Benchmark Contamination in LLMs via Person-Fit Statistics on Binary Response Patterns\n\nThe paper adapts IRT person-fit statistics (ℓz*) to detect benchmark contamination in LLMs using only binary (correct/incorrect) response patterns — the \"minimal-information operating point\" on the detection frontier. 8 pages main text + references.\n\n**Three claimed contributions:**\n1. Minimal-information detection framework using ℓz* validated on 3 model families\n2. Detection characterization: power function (R²=0.62), 56% effect-size cost quantification, preliminary post-RL robustness\n3. Empirical findings: universal 4PL misfit across 12 models (57/60 cells)\n\n**Key method:** Compute ℓz* (Snijders 2001 corrected person-fit statistic) using pre-calibrated 4PL IRT parameters from PSN-IRT. Use Δℓz* (clean vs contaminated shift) as detection statistic. Novel analyses: θ-fixed to restore dose-response monotonicity, memorization fraction (MF), empirical power function.\n\n**Key results:**\n- |d|>2.0 in 2/3 families at 15% MMLU contamination (Qwen 2.27, Llama 2.09), Mistral 1.51\n- 56% effect-size cost vs token-prob methods (2.27 vs 5.20)\n- Post-RL: |d|=0.82 (partial retention) vs Min-K%++/PPL ≈0\n- HT statistic (also binary) achieves |d|=3.21-3.53, outperforming ℓz*\n- Item-level AUROC near-random (0.52-0.55)\n- Power function R²=0.62 across 7 benchmarks\n- Universal 4PL misfit: 57/60 model-benchmark cells flagged\n\n**Related work cited:** Brown et al. 2020 (n-gram), Shi et al. 2024 (Min-K%), Zhang et al. 2024 (Min-K%++), Deng et al. 2024 (Time Travel), Oren et al. 2024 (ConStat), CoDeC 2026, Wang et al. 2025 (post-RL failure), PSN-IRT 2026, IRT-LLM 2024, Drasgow et al. 1985, Snijders 2001, Hong et al. 2021 (multiscale lzm), Meijer & Sijtsma 2001.\n\n## Your Task (R2 — Novelty + Related Work + Presentation)\n\n### 1. Novelty Assessment\n- Is applying IRT person-fit statistics to LLM contamination detection genuinely novel? Or is it a straightforward application of existing psychometric tools to a new domain?\n- The θ-shift analysis — is this a significant methodological contribution or a known issue in person-fit literature?\n- The \"detection frontier\" / \"minimal-information operating point\" framing — does it constitute a conceptual contribution, or is it post-hoc rationalization of weaker performance?\n- The universal 4PL misfit finding (contribution 3) — is this surprising? LLMs are not human test-takers; wouldn't we expect IRT model misfit?\n- How does the novelty compare to what EMNLP 2026 Main expects?\n\n### 2. Related Work Completeness\n- Are there important missing references? Consider:\n  - Other person-fit statistics beyond ℓz* (e.g., U3, W, ECI indices) — some are mentioned as baselines but not thoroughly discussed\n  - IRT applications in NLP/LLM evaluation beyond PSN-IRT and IRT-LLM\n  - Other minimal-information contamination detection approaches\n  - Benchmark integrity / test security literature from educational measurement\n  - Recent contamination detection work (2024-2026)\n- Is Wang et al. [2025] properly contextualized? The post-RL claim heavily depends on this single reference.\n- Are the anonymous citations (PSN-IRT [2026], CoDeC [2026], IRT-LLM [2024]) potential anonymity violations?\n\n### 3. Positioning and Framing\n- The paper positions ℓz* as the primary method despite HT statistic achieving better |d| (3.21-3.53 vs 2.27/2.09) — is the justification (\"mechanistic interpretability\") convincing?\n- \"Minimal-information operating point\" — is this framing too generous? The method requires pre-calibrated 4PL parameters from PSN-IRT, which is itself a non-trivial dependency.\n- The 56% effect-size cost is highlighted as if it's acceptable — but is it? For practical contamination detection, would practitioners choose a weaker method?\n- The post-RL advantage is the strongest selling point, but it's based on N=1, 77% complete — is the paper over-selling this?\n\n### 4. Presentation Quality\n- Is the paper well-written and well-structured?\n- Are the tables and figures (described but I'll assess the textual descriptions) effective?\n- Is the 8-page limit respected? Does content feel compressed or padded?\n- Are claims appropriately hedged vs over-stated?\n- Are limitations adequately discussed?\n\n### 5. Broader Impact / Ethics\n- Any ethical concerns with a contamination detection tool?\n- Responsible NLP checklist considerations?\n\n### 6. Minor Issues\n- Reference formatting: \"PSN-IRT Authors\", \"CoDeC Authors\", \"IRT-LLM Authors\" — these are placeholder names, not proper anonymous citations\n- Any notation inconsistencies?\n\nProvide your assessment in structured format:\n- Summary (3-4 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, labeled W1, W2, ... with severity: major/minor)\n- Questions for Authors\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall Assessment: X/5 (use the scale above)\n- Confidence: X/5\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Calibrate to EMNLP 2026 acceptance rate (~21% Main, ~30% with Findings)."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01J8g3Zf8wCniHTdxWaid28j","session_id":"38461102-0053-4fc0-9aca-3e348094d93e","uuid":"772cc10e-0088-4868-9dec-62ec656c0778","timestamp":"2026-05-21T05:43:27.672Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DQByseUMYWjMmWedqeXdkf","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Background and Related Work}\\label{sec:background}\n2\t\n3\t\\subsection{IRT and Person-Fit Statistics}\\label{sec:irt_background}\n4\t\n5\tItem Response Theory (IRT) models the probability that an examinee answers a test item correctly as a function of the examinee's latent ability $\\theta$ and item-level parameters \\citep{lord1980, embretson2000}.\n6\tThe four-parameter logistic (4PL) model, formalized in \\S\\ref{sec:framework} (Eq.~\\ref{eq:4pl}), extends the classical three-parameter model \\citep{birnbaum1968} with an upper asymptote $d$ that captures the probability of error even at high ability \\citep{barton1981}.\n7\tThis parameter matters for LLMs: \\citet{psnirt2026} find that 46\\% of MMLU items have $d < 0.95$, meaning ceiling effects are pervasive and a model without the $d$ parameter systematically misestimates item characteristics.\n8\t\n9\tGiven calibrated item parameters, person-fit statistics measure whether an examinee's \\emph{pattern} of correct and incorrect responses is consistent with the IRT model at the estimated ability level.\n10\tThe key intuition is that a test-taker with ability $\\theta$ should get hard items wrong and easy items right at rates the model predicts; systematic deviations signal aberrant behavior.\n11\t\\citet{drasgow1985} introduced $\\ell_z$, a standardized log-likelihood residual that aggregates per-item deviations into a single score.\n12\t\\citet{snijders2001} corrected the variance of $\\ell_z$ for uncertainty in the estimated $\\hat{\\theta}$, yielding $\\ell_z^*$ with the property that $\\ell_z^* \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0,1)$ under the null hypothesis of model-consistent responding.\n13\tNegative values indicate \\emph{aberrant} patterns (unexpected errors on easy items or unexpected successes on hard items), while positive values indicate \\emph{over-consistent} patterns (responses too predictable given the model).\n14\tWe defer the formal definition to \\S\\ref{sec:framework}.\n15\t\n16\tIn psychometrics, person-fit statistics have a long history in detecting test fraud and item preknowledge.\n17\t\\citet{karabatsos2003} compared 36 person-fit statistics and found $\\ell_z$ among the most powerful for detecting cheating.\n18\t\\citet{sinharay2017} showed that $\\ell_z^*$ is particularly effective when the compromised item set is known, a setting analogous to benchmark contamination where the ``compromised'' items are those memorized during pretraining.\n19\t\\citet{meijer2001} provide a comprehensive review of the broader person-fit literature.\n20\tOur work transfers this psychometric toolkit to LLM evaluation, treating each model as an examinee whose response pattern may reveal memorization.\n21\t\n22\t\\subsection{Benchmark Contamination Detection}\\label{sec:contamination_related}\n23\t\n24\t\\begin{table}[t]\n25\t\\centering\n26\t\\small\n27\t\\caption{Detection frontier ordered by information access level. RL column: robustness to RL post-training ($\\times$ = fails, -- = untested).}\n28\t\\label{tab:frontier}\n29\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}llc@{}}\n30\t\\toprule\n31\tInformation Required & Method & RL \\\\\n32\t\\midrule\n33\tTraining corpus & N-gram overlap \\citep{dodge2021} & N/A \\\\\n34\tLog-probabilities & Min-K\\%++ \\citep{minkprob2024} & $\\times$\\,$^{a}$ \\\\\n35\tGenerated text & Rephrased probing \\citep{golchin2024} & -- \\\\\n36\tPerf.\\ (multi-form) & ConStat \\citep{constat2024} & -- \\\\\n37\t\\textbf{Binary only} & \\textbf{\\irtfit{} (ours)} & $^{b}$ \\\\\n38\t\\bottomrule\n39\t\\end{tabular}\n40\t\n41\t{\\raggedright\\scriptsize $^{a}$\\citet{wang2025fragility}: 10 methods fail post-RL.\n42\t$^{b}$Structurally invariant; empirical verification future work.\\par}\n43\t\\end{table}\n44\t\n45\tContamination detection methods vary in what information they require from the model under test.\n46\tTable~\\ref{tab:frontier} organizes the detection frontier by information access, from most to least demanding.\n47\tAt the high end, corpus-search methods match benchmark items against pretraining data \\citep{dodge2021, magar2022}, but require full corpus access, which is unavailable for closed models.\n48\tMembership inference attacks (MIA) use token-level log-probabilities to distinguish training examples from unseen ones \\citep{minkprob2024, deng2024}.\n49\tGeneration-based probes elicit benchmark content via prompting \\citep{golchin2024, oren2024}.\n50\tPerformance-based methods such as ConStat \\citep{constat2024} and CoDeC \\citep{codec2026} compare a model's accuracy on the target benchmark against accuracy on perturbed or reference sets, requiring either multiple evaluations or in-context learning passes.\n51\t\\citet{sainz2023} propose overlapping-split analysis using time-stamped data.\n52\t\n53\tA critical limitation unites all log-probability methods: \\citet{wang2025fragility} demonstrate that reinforcement learning (RL) post-training renders MIA-based detectors (including Min-K\\%++) no better than random across 10 methods and 4 benchmarks.\n54\tRL reshapes token-level output distributions while largely preserving binary correctness patterns, so token-level statistics lose signal while binary response patterns remain available for analysis.\n55\t\\citet{selfcritique2026} and \\citet{zhang2026rlvr} propose RL-specific solutions but require generation-level access.\n56\t\n57\t\\irtfit{} operates at the bottom of this frontier: it requires only binary responses (correct/incorrect), making it applicable to any model accessible through a standard evaluation API.\n58\tRecent work applies IRT to LLM evaluation for ability estimation \\citep{martinezplumed2019, polo2024tinybenchmarks}, adaptive testing \\citep{atlas2025}, and benchmark diagnostics \\citep{psnirt2026}, but none use \\emph{person-fit} statistics for contamination detection.\n59\tOur work bridges this gap.\n60\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_013yzA5sjbJcJEm6UAx1yNFb","session_id":"38461102-0053-4fc0-9aca-3e348094d93e","uuid":"3781b8d2-0292-4268-8896-9f0c7065be67","timestamp":"2026-05-21T05:43:27.202Z"}
